**(i) Arranging the features (5 marks).** An **erosional feature** is formed when the agent (river, waves, wind, glacier) wears away and removes material; a **depositional feature** is built up from the material that the agent drops.

| Erosional features (5) | Depositional features (5) |
|---|---|
| **Yardangs** (wind) | **Natural levees** (river) |
| **Cliffs** (waves) | **Deltas** (river and sea) |
| **Hanging valleys** (glacier) | **Barchans** (wind) |
| **Rock pedestals** (wind) | **Drumlins** (glacier) |
| **Gorges** (river) | **Beaches** (waves) |

Half a mark for each correctly placed feature (10 features, 5 marks).

**(ii) Two labelled diagrams (4 marks).** Two examples are described here; any one erosional and one depositional feature with correct labels earns the marks (2 marks for each diagram: shape and labels, and the characteristics).

**Erosional feature: a cliff with a wave-cut platform (coastal).** Draw a side view (cross-section) of the coast, from the sea on the left to the land on the right, and label:

- the **sea** with **waves attacking** the foot of the cliff (arrows);
- the **wave-cut notch** at the base of the cliff, where the waves attack at high tide by hydraulic action and abrasion;
- the **overhanging cliff face** that is undercut and finally collapses (rock falls);
- the **wave-cut platform** (gently sloping rock surface at the foot of the cliff, exposed at low tide), the rock surface left behind as the cliff retreats;
- the **high tide level** and the **low tide level**; the **retreat of the cliff** (arrow towards the land);
- *Characteristics:* a steep, almost vertical face, a notch at its base and a gently sloping platform in front.

**Depositional feature: a barchan (desert).** Draw a plan view (seen from above) with the wind blowing from one side, and label:

- the **direction of the prevailing wind** (arrow);
- the **windward slope**, gentle (about 15 degrees), on which the sand grains are pushed up;
- the **crest**, and the **slip face** (lee slope), steep (about 30 to 35 degrees), on which the sand falls and slides;
- the two **horns** (arms) of the crescent, which **point downwind**;
- *Characteristics:* a crescent-shaped sand dune that migrates slowly in the direction of the wind (a few metres a year), formed where the sand supply is limited and the wind blows from a constant direction, for example in the Sahara.
